# CD50

12 arcade games built from 172 reusable components. Zero game scripts.

CD50 is a modular arcade game collection built in [Godot](https://godotengine.org) using a composable component architecture. Every game — from Pong to Tetris to a Galaga-asteroids mashup — is assembled by attaching generic components to generic entities in the editor. No game-specific code exists anywhere in the project.

---

## The Architecture

### Three Rules

1. **Composition over inheritance.** Entities are blank slates. All behavior comes from attached components.
2. **Signals, not calls.** Components never call methods on other components. They emit signals and let the recipient decide what to do.
3. **Single-purpose components.** Each component does one thing. A Brain generates intent. A Leg moves. An Arm affects the world. A Guts tracks internal state.

### The Pipeline

Every frame, components execute in a deterministic priority cascade:

```
REGISTRATION → INPUT → INTENT → STEERING → PHYSICS → COLLISION → INTERACTION → STATE → VISUAL → AUDIO → RULES → MANAGER → UPDATE
 sync groups   read    "go!"    calc       moves     "you hit    damage       health   draw it   play it   score it   orchestrate  flush
                input            speed +    the       something"  to target    drops    on screen out loud  check win  mutations
                                 direction  thing
```

Lower priority = runs earlier. Brains decide before Legs move. Legs move before Arms react to collisions. Guts track damage after Arms deal it. Faces draw the final state. Stage components update scores and check win conditions last.

This eliminates frame-ordering bugs. Every component knows exactly when it runs relative to every other component, regardless of scene tree position.

### Component Categories

| Category | Priority | Role | Count | Examples |
|----------|----------|------|-------|---------|
| **Brains** | 10 | Generate intent from input or AI | 17 | PlayerMoveBrain, AIChaseBrain, AIFormationBrain, AISwoopBrain |
| **Legs** | 20 | Execute movement from intent signals | 15 | DirectMovementLeg, EngineLeg, GridMovementLeg, ScreenWrapLeg |
| **Arms** | 40 | Affect other entities and game state | 16 | DamageOnHitArm, DeathOnCrashArm, GunArm, ScoreOnCollisionArm |
| **Guts** | 50 | Track internal entity state | 19 | HealthpoolGuts, DeflectorBounceGuts, LockDetectorGuts, StunGuts |
| **Faces** | 60 | Draw entity appearance | 7 | PolygonFace, VectorFace, SpriteFace, DeathEffectFace |
| **Voices** | 65 | Entity-level audio | 2 | SoundVoice, ContinuousVoice |
| **Stage** | 70 | Game-level logic, UI, spawning | 27 | ScoreCard, GroupCountGoal, GridTrapdoor, FormationDirector, MusicSpeaker |
| **Manager** | 75 | Data-driven stage orchestration | 3 | StageManager, StateManager, SignalManager |

Plus 16 core infrastructure scripts (CDEntity, CDGame, CDComponent2D, CDStageComponent2D, CDCollisionBuffer, CDGroupRegistry, etc.) and 47 custom resources (curves, triggers, selectors, shapes, transitions, scalers, formations, wall kicks, sound definitions).

---

## How It Works: Building Pong

Every game is a scene tree of generic nodes. Here's how Paddle Ball (Pong) is assembled:

```
CDGame (game root, state machine)
├── CDGroupRegistry
├── CDCollisionBuffer
├── CDCollisionMatrix
├── ScoreCard (tracks score)
├── LivesCard (tracks lives)
├── GroupCountGoal (triggers victory/defeat when a group empties)
│
├── CDEntity "player_paddle"
│   ├── PlayerMoveBrain (reads input → emits "move" signal)
│   ├── DirectMovementLeg (hears "move" → sets velocity)
│   └── PolygonFace (draws paddle shape)
│
├── CDEntity "ai_paddle"
│   ├── AIChaseBrain (tracks ball position → emits "move" signal)
│   ├── DirectMovementLeg (hears "move" → sets velocity)
│   └── PolygonFace
│
└── CDEntity "ball"
    ├── DeflectorBounceGuts (custom bounce physics off paddles)
    ├── DeathOnCrashArm (self-destructs on wall collision → scores a point)
    ├── DirectMovementLeg (constant velocity)
    └── PolygonFace (draws ball)
```

No Pong script exists. The game emerges from how generic components are wired together via configurable signal names.

---

## How It Works: Remixing into Meteor Rally

The architecture's real power: swapping components creates entirely new games.

Meteor Rally = Pong + asteroids. The paddles, ball, and scoring stay. But now:

- Add asteroid entities: `AIIdleWanderBrain` + `EngineLeg` + `ScreenWrapLeg` + `DamageOnJoustArm` + `PolygonFace`
- Add a `DeathOnCrashArm` to the ball (it dies on asteroid impact)
- Add `HealthpoolGuts` to the player paddle (asteroids deal damage)
- Add a `LivesCard` for the health display

Same base components. Different assembly. New game. This is how CD50 ships 12 games from one component library.

---

## The Signal Bus

Components communicate through two signal systems:

**Entity Bus** — Native Godot signals on each CDEntity, now zero-arg via `bus_emit()`. High-frequency. The hot path: Brains emit `"move"`, Legs consume it. Arms hear `"collision"`, deal damage to the collider's bus. Continuous data flows through `entity.blackboard` (Dictionary).

**Game Bus** — Native Godot signals on CDGame, also zero-arg via `bus_emit()` and `bus_emit_from()`. Data flows through `game.blackboard` (Dictionary). Low-frequency, configurable names. For game-level events: `"score_gained"`, `"wave_cleared"`, `"lives_depleted"`. Zero registration boilerplate — `bus_connect()` auto-creates signals, emit with no listeners is a no-op. `bus_emit_from()` tracks which entity signaled this frame, enabling signal-aware selectors like `CDSelectSignalEmitter`.

This hybrid approach gives type safety where it matters (per-entity, per-frame) and flexibility where it doesn't (game-level events that vary per game).

